# Refract — Game state

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build owns the shape of the game's state entirely. Choose the types, the
fields, and the modules that hold them to suit the implementation you write.

The one contract fixed over the state is the debugging and automation surface
specified for this build. Every operation that surface carries is a read or a
pose of the running game, so the state holds what those operations read, honors
what they write, and returns to its title-screen values when the surface's
`reset` is called. Beyond satisfying that surface, how the state is organized is
yours to decide.

Whatever its shape, the state carries the following:

- The mode the player chose from the title menu, Campaign or Cascade. It decides
  which screens the game moves between and where the next board comes from.
- The item highlighted on whichever vertical menu the current screen shows,
  counted from `0`. The snapshot reports it as `menuIndex`.
- The board being played: its `cols` and `rows`, and every node on it with the
  cell it occupies, its kind, the channel an emitter or a lens belongs to, and a
  crystal's charge count. A node's center on the stage comes from the cell center
  formula in `specs/board.md` rather than from the state.
- One beam per channel present on the board, each carrying the cells it runs
  through in the order they were drawn. Consecutive cells are joined by a
  segment, so a beam of `n` cells carries `n - 1` segments and a beam that has
  been started and not yet extended carries one cell and no segments. The first
  cell is the node the trace that drew it began at, and the last cell is the end
  the most recent trace worked from: whenever a trace begins on a beam, the beam
  is oriented first so that the end being extended is its last cell, reversing
  the stored order when the trace begins at the other end.
- How much of each crystal is spent. A charge is spent the moment a beam enters
  the crystal, so the spent count is the number of times that crystal's cell
  appears across the beams, whether or not the crossing has been left again.
- The live trace, and nothing when no trace is live. A trace carries the channel
  of the beam it is extending, and its live end is the last cell of that beam.
- The pointer position, in the stage's logical units, and whether the pointer is
  currently pressed, both refreshed from the runtime layer in every update.
- The course's progress, as `specs/modes/campaign.md` describes it: which board
  is being played, which boards have been solved, and how many boards are open
  to the player.
- The board highlighted on the campaign's select grid, zero-indexed the same
  way as the course's boards. The snapshot reports it as `selectIndex`.
- The run's progress, as `specs/modes/cascade.md` describes it: how many boards
  have been solved, and the tier the generator emits at.
- The simulation time, in seconds, which every update adds its `dt` to whatever
  the screen, the menus included. The snapshot reports it as `simTime`.
- The state of the seeded random generator, which Cascade draws its boards from.

Both modes' progress is carried whatever the mode being played, so the state has
one shape. The progress of the mode that is not being played holds the values it
carries before that mode is entered.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it stands
here. The state is a value the engine holds and every frame replaces, so every
field and every array is declared `readonly`: a frame builds the next state from
the current one rather than writing into it, and every reader is handed the
state as `DeepReadonly<RefractState>` in any case.

```ts
export type Screen =
  "title" | "howto" | "select" | "playing" | "solved" | "complete";

export type Mode = "campaign" | "cascade";

export type Channel = "triangle" | "square" | "diamond";

export type NodeKind = "emitter" | "lens" | "crystal";

export interface Cell {
  readonly col: number;
  readonly row: number;
}

export interface NodeState {
  readonly col: number;
  readonly row: number;
  readonly kind: NodeKind;
  readonly channel: Channel | null;
  readonly charges: number | null;
}

export interface BoardState {
  readonly cols: number;
  readonly rows: number;
  readonly nodes: readonly NodeState[];
}

export interface BeamState {
  readonly channel: Channel;
  readonly cells: readonly Cell[];
}

export interface TraceState {
  readonly channel: Channel;
}

export interface PointerState {
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
  readonly down: boolean;
}

export interface RefractState {
  readonly screen: Screen;
  readonly mode: Mode;
  readonly menuIndex: number;

  readonly board: BoardState;
  readonly beams: readonly BeamState[];
  readonly tracing: TraceState | null;

  readonly boardIndex: number;
  readonly solvedBoards: readonly number[];
  readonly unlockedCount: number;
  readonly selectIndex: number;

  readonly solvedCount: number;
  readonly tier: number;

  readonly pointer: PointerState;
  readonly simTime: number;
  readonly muted: boolean;
  readonly rngState: number;
}
```

## The type aliases

`Screen` is the top-level state machine, and `screen` names the screen the game
is showing. Every build carries all six, and `select` and `complete` are
reached in Campaign alone, as `specs/modes/campaign.md` describes. `Mode` names
the way play is chosen from the title menu: the fixed course of
`specs/modes/campaign.md` or the endless run of `specs/modes/cascade.md`.
`Channel` names one beam identity, and the three are the members of `CHANNELS`
in that order. `NodeKind` names the three kinds of node `specs/board.md`
defines. `Cell` addresses one cell of the board, `col` and `row` both
zero-indexed from the top-left.

## `NodeState`

One node and the cell it occupies. Only a cell holding a node has an entry, so
an empty cell appears nowhere.

- `col`, `row` — the cell the node occupies.
- `kind` — `"emitter"`, `"lens"`, or `"crystal"`.
- `channel` — the channel an emitter or a lens belongs to, and `null` for a
  crystal, which is channel-neutral.
- `charges` — a crystal's charge count, a whole number from `1` to
  `MAX_CHARGES` (`3`), and `null` for an emitter and for a lens.

Two facts about a node are derived rather than stored. Its center on the stage
is `cellX(col, cols)` and `cellY(row, rows)` from `specs/board.md`, so a board
sits where its dimensions put it and the state carries no coordinates. How much
of a crystal is spent is read off the beams: a charge is spent the moment a beam
enters the crystal, so the spent count is the number of times that crystal's
cell appears across the beams, whether or not the crossing has been left again.

## `BoardState`

The board being played.

- `cols`, `rows` — the grid's dimensions, within `GRID_MAX_COLS` (`7`) by
  `GRID_MAX_ROWS` (`6`).
- `nodes` — every node on the board, in reading order from the top-left cell to
  the bottom-right.

## `BeamState`

One channel's beam.

- `channel` — the channel the beam belongs to.
- `cells` — the cells the beam runs through, in the order they were drawn.
  Consecutive cells are joined by a segment, so a beam of `n` cells carries
  `n - 1` segments, a beam that has been started at an emitter and not yet
  extended carries one cell and no segments, and a beam nobody has touched
  carries none.

A beam's stored order is what makes its two ends distinguishable. The first cell
is the node the trace that drew it began at, and the last cell is the end the
most recent trace worked from. Whenever a trace begins on a beam, the beam is
oriented first so that the end the trace will extend from is its last cell,
reversing the stored order when the trace begins at the other end, so the live
end is always the last cell and a press part way along the beam drops every cell
after it.

`beams` carries one entry per channel present on the board, in the order of
`CHANNELS`, and a channel the board does not use has no entry at all. Whether a
beam is complete is derived from R6 and R7 in `specs/beams.md` rather than
stored, and whether the board is solved is derived from R9.

## `TraceState`

The trace currently live, and `null` whenever none is.

- `channel` — the channel of the beam the trace is extending. The live end is
  the last cell of that channel's beam, so it is read from there rather than
  held twice.

`tracing` becomes `null` on the frame a move solves the board, leaving the beams
exactly as drawn, so the release edge that follows begins nothing and changes
nothing.

## `PointerState`

Where the pointer is and whether it is pressed, as the game read it in the
current frame.

- `x`, `y` — the pointer position in the stage's logical units, the same units
  the cell centers are in, so a hit test against a cell center needs no
  conversion.
- `down` — whether the pointer is currently pressed. A press with no node within
  `NODE_HIT_R` (`44`) of it leaves `down` `true` and `tracing` `null`.

## `RefractState`

The whole of Refract's state. Every field is present from the moment
`initialize` returns, and every one is plain data: numbers, strings, booleans,
and containers of them, so a board is posed by building a state that differs in
the fields posed and read back by reading them.

- `screen` — the screen currently shown.
- `mode` — the mode chosen from the title menu, and the one the board being
  played belongs to.
- `menuIndex` — the highlighted item on whichever vertical menu `screen` is
  showing.
- `board` — the board being played.
- `beams` — one beam per channel present on that board.
- `tracing` — the live trace, or `null`.
- `boardIndex` — the board of the course currently being played, zero-indexed,
  so the first board is `0`.
- `solvedBoards` — the indices of the boards of the course solved so far,
  ascending. Solving a board that is already listed adds nothing.
- `unlockedCount` — how many boards from the start of the course are open to the
  player. It is `1` when the course begins and grows as boards are solved.
- `selectIndex` — the board highlighted on the `select` grid, zero-indexed the
  same way as `boardIndex`.
- `solvedCount` — how many boards of the run have been solved since play began.
- `tier` — the tier the generator emits at, recomputed from `solvedCount` each
  time a board is solved, so it holds the opening tier while `solvedCount` is
  `0`.
- `pointer` — the pointer as of this frame.
- `simTime` — accumulated simulation time, in seconds. Every `update` adds its
  `dt`, whatever the screen, the menus included.
- `muted` — the game's readable copy of the engine's mute bit, which every
  `update` carries into the state it returns. The engine owns muting, and this
  field is what the debug surface reports.
- `rngState` — the state of the game's seeded random generator. A `reset` with a
  seed sets it, so reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same
  result. Cascade draws its boards from it, and Campaign never reads it.

The four course fields and the two run fields are declared whatever `mode`
holds, so the state has one shape. The fields belonging to the mode that is not
being played hold the values they carry before that mode is entered:
`boardIndex` `0`, `solvedBoards` empty, `unlockedCount` `1`, `selectIndex` `0`,
`solvedCount` `0`, and `tier` the opening tier.

## The contract

The declaration above is what the debug surface reads and poses.

- Keep every field under its declared name, type, and meaning.
- `initialize` builds the whole state in one go, so every field is present by
  the time a frame can observe it, and none of them is optional.
- Fields you add hold derived data you can rebuild from the declared ones. A
  `reset` restores the declared fields to their title-screen values, so those
  fields are the whole of the authoritative state.
- The state handed to `update` is the whole of the game, and the state `update`
  returns is the whole of the next frame. Every value the game carries from one
  frame to the next lives in it; nothing is kept in a module-level variable or a
  closure.
- The state is handed over read-only, as `DeepReadonly<RefractState>`, to
  `update`, to `render`, to every diagnostic source, and to every operation of
  the debug surface. `update` and the surface's poses return a new
  `RefractState` built from the one they received; `render`, the sources, and
  `snapshot` return no state.
{{else}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it stands
here. The state is the world's game state: a class extending the engine's
`GameState`, named by the game mode as its `gameStateClass`, so the engine
builds one instance of it when the world opens and `engine.world.state` is that
instance. Wherever this specification writes `state.foo`, it means a field of
that instance.

The game runs in one world for the whole session. The game definition registers
a single level, the game never opens another, and every screen is a value of
`screen`, so the state below is built once and lives until the page closes. The
framework's states are live objects: a tick writes the fields it advances in
place, and each field initializer below is that field's title-screen value, the
same value the debug surface's `reset` restores. `GameState` comes from the
engine's package and `DEFAULT_SEED` from `src/constants.ts`.

```ts
export type Screen =
  "title" | "howto" | "select" | "playing" | "solved" | "complete";

export type Mode = "campaign" | "cascade";

export type Channel = "triangle" | "square" | "diamond";

export type NodeKind = "emitter" | "lens" | "crystal";

export interface Cell {
  col: number;
  row: number;
}

export interface NodeState {
  col: number;
  row: number;
  kind: NodeKind;
  channel: Channel | null;
  charges: number | null;
}

export interface BoardState {
  cols: number;
  rows: number;
  nodes: NodeState[];
}

export interface BeamState {
  channel: Channel;
  cells: Cell[];
}

export interface TraceState {
  channel: Channel;
}

export interface PointerState {
  x: number;
  y: number;
  down: boolean;
}

export class RefractState extends GameState {
  screen: Screen = "title";
  mode: Mode = "campaign";
  menuIndex = 0;

  board: BoardState = { cols: 0, rows: 0, nodes: [] };
  beams: BeamState[] = [];
  tracing: TraceState | null = null;

  boardIndex = 0;
  solvedBoards: number[] = [];
  unlockedCount = 1;
  selectIndex = 0;

  solvedCount = 0;
  tier = 1;

  pointer: PointerState = { x: 0, y: 0, down: false };
  simTime = 0;
  muted = false;
  rngState = DEFAULT_SEED;
}
```

